#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""sapa gate — the mechanical half of running a stream's quality gate.

Running the gate is two jobs. Walking the configured steps, handing each `run:`
step the diff contract, and materializing the plan is mechanical and identical
every time; invoking a skill, honouring its `model:` pin, and deciding whether a
failure is a mechanical fix or an escalation is not. This helper owns only the
mechanical half. The `sapa-gate` skill runs it and reasons about what it emits.

That split is the same one `sapa watch` makes, and it exists for the same reason:
the gate is the phase nothing downstream re-verifies — `sapa-submit` ships on its
word — so its step execution belongs in one tested place rather than being
re-derived from prose every session.

The gate resolves the worktree holding the caller before anything else, and
everything hangs off that one anchor: the config walk-up starts there, and so do
the scratch directory, the stream's issue, the diff against the base, and each
`run:` step's cwd. So a `run:` command's relative paths mean the same thing
wherever inside the tree the gate was invoked from, and a `.sapa.yaml` sitting
above several worktrees covers all of them rather than pulling every step into
the directory it happens to sit in. A caller outside a work tree is refused, not
fallen back on.

Output (one structured line per result, tab-separated, always led by `sapa-gate`
so a step's own output streaming through the same stdout can't be mistaken for
one):

    sapa-gate  plan         <absolute-path>  present|absent
    sapa-gate  step         <name>  run    <exit>     <seconds>
    sapa-gate  step         <name>  skill  pass|fail  -
    sapa-gate  needs-skill  <name>  <skill>  <model-or-->
    sapa-gate  attempts     <spent>  <max>
    sapa-gate  done         green
    sapa-gate  list         <name>  <kind>  <command-or-skill>  <model-or-->  <when>

A `skill:` step needs the agent harness, so the helper stops when it reaches one
rather than trying to run it: it emits `needs-skill` and exits 4. The skill
invokes it and resumes the walk with `--after <name>`, carrying the step's outcome
back on the same call as `--result`/`--summary`/`--fell-back`. Stopping (instead of running every
`run:` step in one pass and reporting the skill steps at the end) is what keeps the
configured order and the fail-fast behaviour intact — a gate that declares `review`
before `test` must not silently become `test` then `review`.

The gate record
---------------

A green gate used to be a sentence in a chat log. The walk now writes what it did
to `$(sapa tmp)/gate-record.json`, and `--report` renders that into the `## Gates`
section `sapa-submit` puts on the PR, so a reviewer can see which axes ran instead
of taking the word of the session that ran them.

The file is `{"runs": [...]}`, appended to rather than overwritten. A bare
invocation appends a run;
`--after` adds steps to the last one. Appending rather than overwriting is what lets
a later gate of a narrower scope sit beside the run that had the full one (#99), and
the report reads the last run. Old runs age out past MAX_RUNS.

Each run stamps the head and base SHAs it gated, its scope (always `full` here),
which fix attempt it is checking, its result, and which of four states the plan
lookup landed in. Each step records
its name, kind, command or skill, model, schedule, whether it fell back off an
unreachable pinned model, result, duration, and a tail of its output.

A `run:` step's result is an exit code the helper watched. A `skill:` step's is what
the agent reported on the resume call, and is stored (and printed) marked as such,
because collapsing the two into one undifferentiated "passed" is the thing this
record exists to stop.

`--after` whose record is missing, unparseable, or whose current run does not cover
the steps before the resume point ignores the resume point and walks from the top.
Redoing a step is cheaper than publishing a record that understates the gate.

Capturing a tail means reading a step's output rather than letting it write to our
descriptors, which would cost every gate run its colors and in-place progress. So
each of stdout and stderr gets a pty when our matching stream is a terminal and a
plain pipe when it is not, and the two stay separate on the way out.

Every `run:` step is given the diff against what will merge, as two environment
variables:

    SAPA_BASE           the branch the PR targets (config `base`, default main)
    SAPA_CHANGED_FILES  newline-separated paths from
                        `git diff --name-only <remote>/<base>...HEAD`

This is the one thing sapa knows and a shell script would reinvent — every script
that recomputed the base did so slightly wrong. A base ref that won't resolve is a
warning, not a failure: the variables come through empty and the gate still runs.

`skill:` steps get the accepted plan as their spec source instead. When any
remains, the plan comment is materialized once to `$(sapa tmp)/plan.md` and its
absolute path emitted with a `present`/`absent` marker. `absent` (the read helper
exits 3, or `gh` can't be reached) means no file is written, which is how
plan-absent stays distinguishable from plan-empty; a read that failed for some
other reason says why on stderr, so the skill can report the difference between
"no plan recorded" and "could not check". It never fails the gate: `sapa gate` is
legitimately runnable before a plan is recorded. A gate with no `skill:` steps
left makes no plan lookup at all.

The autofix budget
------------------

"A step failed, so fix it and run the gate again" has no stop in it. Left
unbounded, a model that keeps almost-fixing one failing step cycles for a long
time, and every pass pays the whole gate including a pinned review step, in the
foreground, with the developer waiting on it. `gate.max_fix_attempts:` (default
3) bounds it, the way `watch.max_ci_fix_attempts` bounds the CI loop for the same
reason.

An episode is one `/sapa-gate`. A bare invocation opens one at attempt 0. A re-run
that carries `--fix-attempt` is sapa saying "this checks a fix I decided on myself"
and takes the next attempt number, which rides on the run entry so it survives
between the separate processes an episode spans. A green run ends the episode, and
the count starts over after it.

Only sapa's own guesses spend from the budget. A fix the developer dictated after
an escalation re-runs bare, because what this bounds is sapa cycling unsupervised,
and a loop blocked on a human answer cannot run away. The bound binds twice: every
failed run emits an `attempts` line so the skill can stop before doing the work of
a fix it will not be allowed to use, and a `--fix-attempt` past the budget is
refused outright, which is what holds when the skill reads the line and carries on.

Exit codes:

    0  every remaining step passed (or `--list`/`--report` printed and exited)
    1  a step failed — a `run:` step's own exit, or a `skill:` step the agent
       resumed with `--result fail`. The walk stops there either way.
    2  a config or usage error — a caller outside a git worktree, no config, no
       `gate:` map, no `gate.steps:`, a malformed step or budget, an unknown
       `--after` name, `--result`/`--summary`/`--fell-back` without `--after` or
       against a `run:` step, `--fell-back` on an unpinned `skill:` step,
       `--fix-attempt` with `--after`, or PyYAML missing. `--report` reads no
       config, so of these it raises only the first
    4  stopped at a `skill:` step awaiting dispatch
    5  the autofix budget is spent, so a `--fix-attempt` ran nothing

Usage:
    sapa gate                  # run the gate from the top
    sapa gate --fix-attempt    # ...re-checking a fix sapa applied itself
    sapa gate --after NAME     # resume with the step after NAME (a handled skill)
      --result pass|fail       #   what that skill step reported (default pass)
      --summary TEXT           #   one line on what it found
      --fell-back              #   its pinned model was unreachable; it ran on the
                               #   session model, and the report says so
    sapa gate --list           # print the resolved steps, run nothing
    sapa gate --report         # print the `## Gates` section, run nothing
    sapa gate --start DIR      # resolve the worktree from DIR instead of $PWD

The config is located by `sapa config`, walking up from the worktree root, so
discovery is the same walk-up every other command does — seeded at the tree being
gated rather than at the caller's cwd, so the config that supplies the steps and
the directory they run in can never come apart.
`git` and `gh` are assumed installed, as everywhere else in
sapa; PyYAML is needed here because a nested list of step maps is past what the
grep-the-printed-config reads elsewhere in sapa can do honestly.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime
import fcntl
import json
import os
import pty
import re
import select
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import termios
import time

from sapa_config import ProjectConfigError, load_mapping

# Every structured line starts with this. `sapa watch` can lead with a bare event
# type because nothing else writes to its stdout; the gate streams a test suite's
# output through the same channel, so its lines need a marker of their own.
MARKER = "sapa-gate"

EXIT_OK = 0
EXIT_STEP_FAILED = 1
EXIT_CONFIG = 2
EXIT_NEEDS_SKILL = 4
EXIT_ATTEMPTS_SPENT = 5

BIN = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))

# The autofix budget when `gate.max_fix_attempts:` is absent. Mirrors watch's
# `max_ci_fix_attempts`, and for the same reason: a fix-and-rerun loop with no
# stop keeps guessing long past the point where the failure is deeper than the
# patch. Three is enough for a typo and short of a grind.
DEFAULT_FIX_ATTEMPTS = 3

# The record lives beside the materialized plan in the stream's scratch directory.
RECORD_NAME = "gate-record.json"

# How many runs the record keeps. A long-lived stream re-gates many times and the
# file is scratch, not history, so old runs age out rather than growing forever.
MAX_RUNS = 20

# How much of a step's output the record keeps. Two caps, whichever bites first,
# so neither a chatty suite nor one enormous line turns the record into a log.
TAIL_LINES = 40
TAIL_CHARS = 4000
TAIL_KEEP_BYTES = 65536  # raw bytes held while streaming, trimmed down at the end

WHEN_ALWAYS = "always"
WHEN_PRE_PUSH = "pre-push"
STEP_WHENS = (WHEN_ALWAYS, WHEN_PRE_PUSH)

# CSI sequences and the shorter two-byte escapes. Colors are worth having on the
# terminal and worth not having in a JSON field a human reads.
ANSI = re.compile(rb"\x1b\[[0-9;?]*[ -/]*[@-~]|\x1b[@-Z\\-_]")


class ConfigError(Exception):
    """A problem with the config or the arguments: reported, then exit 2."""


def emit(*fields) -> None:
    sys.stdout.write("\t".join([MARKER] + [str(f) for f in fields]) + "\n")
    sys.stdout.flush()


def run_helper(name: str, *args: str) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
    """Run a sibling helper from bin/, the way the dispatcher calls them: by path.

    Returns (exit code, raw stdout, stripped stderr). stdout is left untouched
    because one caller writes it to a file and a strip would silently reshape the
    plan. A helper that can't start at all comes back as exit 127 with the reason
    in stderr, so every caller handles one failure shape instead of two.
    """
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [os.path.join(BIN, name), *args],
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True,
        )
    except OSError as exc:
        return 127, "", str(exc)
    return proc.returncode, proc.stdout or "", (proc.stderr or "").strip()


def find_config(start: str) -> str:
    """The discovered `.sapa.yaml` path, via `sapa config` so discovery is shared."""
    code, out, err = run_helper("sapa-config", "--start", start)
    path = out.strip()
    if code != 0 or not path:
        raise ConfigError(err or f"no .sapa.yaml found in any parent of {start}")
    return path


def worktree_root(start: str) -> str:
    """The git worktree holding `start`: the one anchor described at the top of this
    file, resolved before anything else so everything else can hang off it.

    Anchoring on the config's directory instead is what #117 reported. In the
    `.bare` layout the config sits above the worktrees, beside a `.bare` directory
    and nothing else, so every `run:` step executed against no project and the
    branch read as the bare repo's HEAD rather than the worktree's.

    A caller not inside a work tree is refused rather than fallen back on. The one
    place that happens in practice is that same container directory, where falling
    back to the caller would reproduce the exact failure this anchor exists to
    prevent, and would do it while reporting a green gate.
    """
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
            cwd=start, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True,
        )
    except OSError as exc:
        raise ConfigError(f"could not run git to find the worktree holding {start} ({exc})")
    root = (proc.stdout or "").strip()
    if proc.returncode != 0 or not root:
        raise ConfigError(
            f"{start} is not inside a git worktree, so there is no tree to gate. Run "
            f"the gate from the worktree you want checked; in a `.bare` layout that is "
            f"one of the branch directories, not the directory holding `.bare`")
    return root


def gate_map(cfg: dict, path: str) -> dict:
    """The `gate:` mapping. Raises ConfigError when the key is missing or not a map.

    `gate:` used to be the step list itself. It became a map so the gate's own
    settings have somewhere to live beside the steps, and the old shape is rejected
    with the edit that fixes it rather than accepted alongside: carrying both
    shapes is how both end up in the wild.
    """
    gate = cfg.get("gate")
    if gate is None:
        raise ConfigError(f"{path} has no `gate:` key, so there are no steps to run")
    if isinstance(gate, list):
        raise ConfigError(
            f"`gate:` in {path} is a list of steps. It is now a map that holds them "
            f"under `steps:`, so add a `steps:` line under `gate:` and indent the "
            f"existing list one level beneath it")
    if not isinstance(gate, dict):
        raise ConfigError(f"`gate:` in {path} is not a mapping of gate settings")
    return gate


def fix_budget(gate: dict, path: str) -> int:
    """The autofix budget from `gate.max_fix_attempts:`, default DEFAULT_FIX_ATTEMPTS.

    `0` is legal and means never autofix: a repo that always wants to be asked.
    Anything that is not a whole number of attempts is a config error rather than
    something to round or clamp, because reading `-1` or `three` as the default
    would hand back a bound the config never asked for, and the whole point of the
    key is that the developer chose the number.
    """
    raw = gate.get("max_fix_attempts")
    if raw is None:
        return DEFAULT_FIX_ATTEMPTS
    # bool is an int subclass, and YAML turns `yes` into one. `max_fix_attempts:
    # yes` is a mistake, not a budget of 1.
    if isinstance(raw, bool) or not isinstance(raw, int) or raw < 0:
        raise ConfigError(f"`gate.max_fix_attempts:` in {path} is {raw!r}; it takes a "
                          f"whole number of attempts, 0 or more")
    return raw


def resolve_steps(gate: dict, path: str) -> list[dict]:
    """The ordered gate steps, normalized. Raises ConfigError on any bad shape.

    Names must be unique because `--after` resolves the resume point by name;
    two steps called `test` would make a resume ambiguous.
    """
    raw_steps = gate.get("steps")
    if not isinstance(raw_steps, list) or not raw_steps:
        raise ConfigError(f"`gate.steps:` in {path} is not a non-empty list of steps")

    steps = []
    seen = set()
    saw_pre_push = False
    for i, raw in enumerate(raw_steps, 1):
        where = f"gate step {i} in {path}"
        if not isinstance(raw, dict):
            raise ConfigError(f"{where} is not a mapping")
        name = raw.get("name")
        if not name or not isinstance(name, str):
            raise ConfigError(f"{where} has no `name:`")
        if name in seen:
            raise ConfigError(f"duplicate gate step name {name!r} in {path}")
        seen.add(name)
        run, skill = raw.get("run"), raw.get("skill")
        if run and skill:
            raise ConfigError(f"gate step {name!r} has both `run:` and `skill:`")
        if not run and not skill:
            raise ConfigError(f"gate step {name!r} has neither `run:` nor `skill:`")
        if run:
            kind, target = "run", str(run)
        else:
            kind, target = "skill", skill_step_target(skill, name, path)
        when = raw["when"] if "when" in raw else WHEN_ALWAYS
        if not isinstance(when, str) or when not in STEP_WHENS:
            raise ConfigError(
                f"gate step {name!r} has `when: {when}`; expected "
                f"`{WHEN_ALWAYS}` or `{WHEN_PRE_PUSH}`"
            )
        if when == WHEN_PRE_PUSH:
            saw_pre_push = True
        elif saw_pre_push:
            raise ConfigError(
                f"gate step {name!r} has `when: {WHEN_ALWAYS}` after a "
                f"`{WHEN_PRE_PUSH}` step; put every `{WHEN_ALWAYS}` step before "
                f"the first `{WHEN_PRE_PUSH}` step"
            )
        steps.append({
            "name": name,
            "kind": kind,
            "target": target,
            "model": str(raw.get("model") or ""),
            "when": when,
        })
    return steps


def skill_step_target(skill, step_name: str, path: str) -> str:
    """The skill a `skill:` step invokes. It may be a bare name (`code-review`)
    or, when the skill is provisioned by `sapa skills`, an object that carries
    its own source and pinned SHA; then the invocation name is its `name:` or the
    last component of its `path:`. Kept in step with sapa-skills' own name rule."""
    if isinstance(skill, str):
        return skill
    if isinstance(skill, dict):
        name = skill.get("name")
        if isinstance(name, str) and name:
            return name
        p = skill.get("path")
        if isinstance(p, str):
            base = os.path.basename(p.rstrip("/"))
            if base and base != ".":
                return base
        raise ConfigError(
            f"gate step {step_name!r} skill needs a resolvable name "
            f"(a `name:` or a `path:` whose last component names it) in {path}"
        )
    raise ConfigError(
        f"gate step {step_name!r} has a `skill:` that is neither a name nor a mapping in {path}"
    )


def remaining_steps(steps: list[dict], after: str | None) -> list[dict]:
    if not after:
        return steps
    names = [s["name"] for s in steps]
    if after not in names:
        raise ConfigError(f"--after {after!r} names no gate step (have: {', '.join(names)})")
    return steps[names.index(after) + 1:]


def changed_files(remote: str, base: str, cwd: str) -> str:
    """Paths this branch changed against the merge-base, newline-separated.

    A base ref that won't resolve (never fetched, renamed, a gate run outside a
    tracking branch) warns and yields an empty string. The contract already says
    an empty value is legitimate — the branch matching the base produces one —
    so a script handling that case handles this one, and the gate still runs.
    """
    ref = f"{remote}/{base}"
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "diff", "--name-only", f"{ref}...HEAD"],
            cwd=cwd, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True,
        )
    except OSError as exc:
        sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: could not run git ({exc}); SAPA_CHANGED_FILES will be empty\n")
        return ""
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        detail = (proc.stderr or "").strip().splitlines()
        sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: could not diff against {ref} "
                         f"({detail[-1] if detail else 'git failed'}); "
                         f"SAPA_CHANGED_FILES will be empty\n")
        return ""
    return (proc.stdout or "").strip("\n")


def scratch_dir(cwd: str) -> str:
    """This stream's scratch directory, via `sapa tmp` so the keying is shared."""
    code, out, err = run_helper("sapa-tmp", "--start", cwd)
    if code != 0 or not out.strip():
        raise ConfigError(f"could not resolve a scratch directory: {err or 'sapa tmp failed'}")
    return out.strip()


def materialize_plan(cwd: str, scratch: str) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Write the recorded plan comment to `<scratch>/plan.md`. Returns (path, state).

    The state is one of `present`, `absent`, or `unreadable`, and the difference
    between the last two matters: exit 3 from the read helper means no plan comment
    is recorded, while any other failure (no `gh`, no network, not a stream) means
    the gate could not find out. Nothing is written in either case, so a caller that
    only checks for the file can't mistake an unrecorded plan for an empty one.

    Neither one fails the gate — running before a plan is recorded is legitimate —
    but both have to reach the record and the report, because "no plan exists" and
    "could not check" call for different follow-up. The emitted line still collapses
    the two to `absent`, which is all the skill needs to decide what to warn about.
    """
    path = os.path.join(scratch, "plan.md")

    code, out, err = run_helper("sapa-issue", "plan-comment", "--read", "--start", cwd)
    if code == 3:
        return path, "absent"
    if code != 0:
        sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: could not read the plan comment "
                         f"({err or 'sapa issue failed'})\n")
        return path, "unreadable"
    try:
        with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(out)
    except OSError as exc:
        sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: could not write {path} ({exc})\n")
        return path, "unreadable"
    return path, "present"


def git_sha(ref: str, cwd: str) -> str | None:
    """The commit `ref` names, or None when it won't resolve (never fetched, renamed).

    None is a legitimate value in the record: a gate run against a base the machine
    has never fetched is still a gate run, and the report says the SHA is unknown
    rather than pretending to one.
    """
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{ref}^{{commit}}"],
            cwd=cwd, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True,
        )
    except OSError:
        return None
    return (proc.stdout or "").strip() or None


def load_record(path: str) -> dict | None:
    """The record at `path`, or None when there isn't a usable one.

    Missing and unparseable collapse to the same answer on purpose: the one caller
    that asks is a resume looking for a run to append to, and neither case gives it
    one. Both take the same recovery, which is to walk from the top.
    """
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            data = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    if not isinstance(data, dict) or not isinstance(data.get("runs"), list):
        return None
    return {"runs": [r for r in data["runs"] if isinstance(r, dict)]}


def save_record(path: str, record: dict) -> None:
    """Write the record atomically, oldest runs dropped past the cap.

    A write that fails is a warning, never a gate failure: the gate's job is
    checking the work, and losing the record costs disclosure rather than
    correctness. `sapa gate --report` says no record was found, which is true.
    """
    record["runs"] = record["runs"][-MAX_RUNS:]
    tmp = path + ".tmp"
    try:
        with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            json.dump(record, f, indent=2)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError as exc:
        sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: could not write the gate record to {path} ({exc})\n")


def attempts_spent(record: dict | None) -> int:
    """Fix attempts already made in the episode the record's last run belongs to.

    An episode is one `/sapa-gate`: the run that opened it plus every fix sapa
    applied and re-ran on its own. The count has to survive between them because
    each re-run is a separate process, so it rides on the run entry.

    A green run ends its episode, so the count starts over after one. Without that,
    a stream that went green at attempt 2 would carry a spent budget into whatever
    came next, and the developer would be told to stop after a single fix.
    """
    runs = (record or {}).get("runs") or []
    if not runs or runs[-1].get("result") == "green":
        return 0
    spent = runs[-1].get("attempt")
    return spent if isinstance(spent, int) and spent >= 0 else 0


def new_run(cwd: str, base: str, remote: str, attempt: int) -> dict:
    """A fresh run entry, stamped with what it is about to gate.

    `attempt` is which fix in the current episode this run is checking: 0 for the
    run that opened the episode, 1 for the first fix sapa applied and re-ran, and
    so on. It is what the budget is counted against.

    `scope` is always `full` here. It exists because #99 adds a path that gates only
    the `run:` steps before pushing a CI fix, and the report has to distinguish a
    commit that got the whole gate from one that got the mechanical subset.
    """
    # Microseconds kept: two runs of a fast gate can land in the same second, and
    # the timestamp is what orders them for a human reading the file.
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
    return {
        "started": now.isoformat().replace("+00:00", "Z"),
        "scope": "full",
        "attempt": attempt,
        "result": "incomplete",
        "base_ref": f"{remote}/{base}",
        "base_sha": git_sha(f"{remote}/{base}", cwd),
        "head_sha": git_sha("HEAD", cwd),
        "spec_source": "not-looked-up",
        "steps": [],
    }


def resumable(record: dict | None, steps: list[dict], after: str,
              head: str | None) -> bool:
    """True when the record's current run really covers everything before `after`.

    A resume appends to the last run, so that run has to be the one that stopped at
    this skill step: still incomplete, and holding exactly the steps the config puts
    ahead of the resume point. No record at all, a run that already finished, or a
    run from a different config would each leave a record understating what the gate
    covered, and a record that understates the gate is the failure this whole file
    exists to prevent. Walking again costs one repeated step; the alternative costs
    the truth.
    """
    if not record or not record.get("runs"):
        return False
    run = record["runs"][-1]
    if run.get("result") != "incomplete":
        return False
    if run.get("head_sha") != head:
        return False
    names = [s["name"] for s in steps]
    expected = [(s["name"], s["when"]) for s in steps[:names.index(after)]]
    recorded = [(s.get("name"), s.get("when", WHEN_ALWAYS))
                for s in run.get("steps", [])]
    return recorded == expected


def skill_entry(step: dict, result: str | None, summary: str | None,
                fell_back: bool = False) -> dict:
    """The record entry for a `skill:` step, built from what the agent reported back.

    `reported_by` is the point of the whole thing. The helper never ran this step, so
    its result is an assertion where a `run:` step's exit code is an observation, and
    a record that presented the two as the same kind of claim would be the problem it
    was written to fix. No duration is invented either, because nothing measured one.

    A resume that says nothing counts as a reported pass: continuing the walk is the
    agent's judgement that the step passed, and it is honest to record that as what
    it is rather than leave the step out of the account entirely.

    `fell_back` records that the pinned model was unreachable and the step ran on the
    session model instead. `model` stays the pin, because that is what the config
    asked for; the flag is what keeps the report from asserting the pin ran when it
    did not, the same disclosure the record exists for.
    """
    return {
        "name": step["name"],
        "kind": "skill",
        "target": step["target"],
        "model": step["model"] or None,
        "when": step["when"],
        "fell_back": fell_back,
        "result": result or "pass",
        "summary": summary or None,
        "reported_by": "agent",
        "duration": None,
    }


def fail_run(record: dict, path: str, cwd: str, budget: int) -> int:
    """Close the current run as failed and say where the autofix budget stands.

    The attempts line goes out on every failure, not only the one that exhausts the
    budget, so the skill can stop before diagnosing and writing a fix it will not be
    allowed to re-run. The refusal in `gate` is what makes the bound hold when it
    reads this and carries on anyway.

    The count comes off the run being closed rather than from the caller, because a
    `--after` resume closes a run it did not open: the attempt is whatever the
    invocation that started the walk stamped, which only the record knows.
    """
    finish_run(record, path, cwd, "failed")
    emit("attempts", record["runs"][-1].get("attempt", 0), budget)
    return EXIT_STEP_FAILED


def finish_run(record: dict, path: str, cwd: str, result: str) -> None:
    """Close the current run with its outcome and the head it actually finished on."""
    run = record["runs"][-1]
    run["result"] = result
    # Re-stamp the head: a `skill:` step earlier in the walk may have committed a
    # fix, and the commit worth recording is the one the gate ended on.
    run["head_sha"] = git_sha("HEAD", cwd) or run.get("head_sha")
    save_record(path, record)


def tail_of(raw: bytes) -> str:
    """The end of a step's output, decoded and cleaned up for the record.

    Escapes come out, and a line rewritten in place by carriage returns collapses to
    what a terminal would actually be showing, so a progress bar contributes one line
    rather than four hundred.
    """
    text = ANSI.sub(b"", raw).decode("utf-8", "replace").replace("\r\n", "\n")
    lines = [line.rsplit("\r", 1)[-1] for line in text.split("\n")[-TAIL_LINES:]]
    return "\n".join(lines)[-TAIL_CHARS:].strip("\n")


def match_window_size(fd: int) -> None:
    """Size a pty to our own terminal, so a step's output wraps where it should."""
    try:
        size = os.get_terminal_size(sys.stdout.fileno())
        fcntl.ioctl(fd, termios.TIOCSWINSZ, struct.pack("HHHH", size.lines, size.columns, 0, 0))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass


def channel(stream) -> tuple[int, int]:
    """(child fd, our fd) for one output channel of a step. Decided per stream.

    A pty when our matching stream is a terminal, so the step keeps the colors and
    in-place progress it would have had if it were talking to the terminal directly.
    A plain pipe when it is not, because handing a redirected gate's child a fake
    terminal buys nothing and fills the log with progress-bar spam.

    Reading the output at all is what forces this choice: capturing a tail means the
    step can no longer write straight to our descriptors.
    """
    if stream.isatty():
        ours, child = pty.openpty()
        match_window_size(ours)
        return child, ours
    read_fd, write_fd = os.pipe()
    return write_fd, read_fd


def pump(sources: dict) -> bytearray:
    """Stream every source through to its own output, keeping a copy of the tail.

    stdout and stderr stay separate on the way out, the way they were when steps
    wrote to our descriptors directly, so a caller redirecting one of them still gets
    what it expects. The kept copy interleaves them in read order, which is what
    makes a tail worth reading.
    """
    kept = bytearray()
    remaining = dict(sources)
    while remaining:
        try:
            ready, _, _ = select.select(list(remaining), [], [])
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            break
        for fd in ready:
            try:
                chunk = os.read(fd, 4096)
            except OSError:
                chunk = b""  # EIO: a pty whose child just closed the last slave fd
            if not chunk:
                remaining.pop(fd, None)
                continue
            remaining[fd].write(chunk)
            remaining[fd].flush()
            kept.extend(chunk)
            del kept[:-TAIL_KEEP_BYTES]
    return kept


def capture(cmd: str, cwd: str, env: dict) -> tuple[int, str]:
    """Run `cmd`, stream its output through ours, and return (exit code, tail)."""
    out_child, out_ours = channel(sys.stdout)
    err_child, err_ours = channel(sys.stderr)
    try:
        sys.stdout.flush()
        sys.stderr.flush()
        try:
            proc = subprocess.Popen(cmd, shell=True, cwd=cwd, env=env,
                                    stdout=out_child, stderr=err_child)
        finally:
            os.close(out_child)
            os.close(err_child)
        kept = pump({out_ours: sys.stdout.buffer, err_ours: sys.stderr.buffer})
        return proc.wait(), tail_of(kept)
    finally:
        for fd in (out_ours, err_ours):
            try:
                os.close(fd)
            except OSError:
                pass


def run_step(step: dict, cwd: str, base: str, changed: str) -> dict:
    """Run one `run:` step under the env contract, emit its result line, return its record entry."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["SAPA_BASE"] = base
    env["SAPA_CHANGED_FILES"] = changed
    started = time.monotonic()
    try:
        code, tail = capture(step["target"], cwd, env)
    except OSError as exc:
        sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: could not run step {step['name']!r} ({exc})\n")
        code, tail = 127, ""
    duration = round(time.monotonic() - started, 1)
    emit("step", step["name"], "run", code, f"{duration:.1f}")
    return {
        "name": step["name"],
        "kind": "run",
        "target": step["target"],
        "model": step["model"] or None,
        "when": step["when"],
        "result": "pass" if code == 0 else "fail",
        "exit": code,
        "duration": duration,
        "tail": tail,
    }


NO_RECORD = ("No gate record was found for this branch, so there is no account of what "
             "sapa checked. The record is written by `sapa gate` and lives in the "
             "stream's scratch directory, which does not survive a reboot.")

# What the report says about the plan, per state. Each states what sapa observed
# rather than what it concludes: sapa cannot tell whether a `skill:` step is a spec
# review or whether a `run:` step is not, so a line claiming spec compliance went
# unchecked would be a verdict on the repo's gate that sapa is not entitled to give.
SPEC_SOURCE_LINES = {
    "present": "Reviewed against the plan recorded on the issue.",
    "absent": "No plan is recorded on the issue, so no step was given one as a spec source.",
    "unreadable": "The recorded plan could not be read, so no step was given one as a "
                  "spec source.",
    "not-looked-up": "No step was given the recorded plan as a spec source.",
}


def short(sha: str | None) -> str | None:
    return sha[:7] if sha else None


def render_step(step: dict) -> str:
    """One bullet for one step: what it was called, what kind it was, how it went.

    Names, never commands. A step's name is a label its author chose and its command
    is the fact, but the config is checked in and shows up in the diff, so a reviewer
    who wants the command has it without the report carrying a 500-character chain.
    """
    name = step.get("name", "?")
    schedule = (" (pre-push)"
                if step.get("when") == WHEN_PRE_PUSH else "")
    passed = step.get("result") == "pass"
    if step.get("kind") == "skill":
        what = f"skill `{step.get('target', '?')}`"
        model = step.get("model")
        if model and step.get("fell_back"):
            # The pin was unreachable, so the step ran on the session model. Say both,
            # rather than let `on <pin>` assert a model that never ran the review.
            what += f", pinned `{model}` unreachable, ran on the session model"
        elif model:
            what += f" on `{model}`"
        # Said out loud on every skill bullet: the helper did not run this step, so
        # "passed" here is the agent's report, not an exit code sapa watched.
        return (f"- **{name}**{schedule}: {what}, "
                f"{'passed' if passed else 'failed'}, agent-reported")
    duration = step.get("duration")
    secs = f"{duration:.1f}s" if isinstance(duration, (int, float)) else ""
    if passed:
        return f"- **{name}**{schedule}: command, passed" + (f" in {secs}" if secs else "")
    return (f"- **{name}**{schedule}: command, failed (exit {step.get('exit', '?')})"
            + (f" after {secs}" if secs else ""))


def render_run(run: dict, head: str | None) -> list[str]:
    """The report for one run: what it gated, what ran, and anything qualifying it."""
    gated = short(run.get("head_sha"))
    base_ref = run.get("base_ref") or "the base"
    base_sha = short(run.get("base_sha"))
    against = f"`{base_ref}@{base_sha}`" if base_sha else f"`{base_ref}` (unresolved)"
    out = [f"Gated {f'`{gated}`' if gated else 'an unrecorded commit'} against {against}.", ""]

    steps = run.get("steps") or []
    out.extend(render_step(s) for s in steps)
    if not steps:
        out.append("- no steps ran")
    out.append("")

    notes = []
    if run.get("result") == "failed":
        notes.append("This run did not go green.")
    elif run.get("result") == "incomplete":
        notes.append("This run never finished, so the list above is partial.")
    if gated and head and run.get("head_sha") != head:
        notes.append(f"The gate ran on `{gated}`, which is not the current head "
                     f"`{short(head)}`.")
    notes.append(SPEC_SOURCE_LINES.get(run.get("spec_source"),
                                       SPEC_SOURCE_LINES["not-looked-up"]))
    out.extend(notes)
    return out


def render_record(record: dict | None, head: str | None) -> str:
    """The whole `## Gates` section, ready to paste into a PR body.

    Only the last run is reported. #99 adds runs that gate the `run:` steps alone
    before pushing a CI fix, and reporting the earlier ones alongside it is that
    issue's job; the split between this and `render_run` is where it goes.
    """
    out = ["## Gates", ""]
    runs = (record or {}).get("runs") or []
    if runs:
        out.extend(render_run(runs[-1], head))
    else:
        out.append(NO_RECORD)
    return "\n".join(out).rstrip("\n") + "\n"


def report(start: str) -> int:
    """Print the `## Gates` section for this stream's record.

    Always exit 0 with a section on stdout. A missing record is a normal state, not
    an error: `/sapa-submit` runs on branches gated in an earlier session, and the
    honest answer there is a section saying so rather than a failure the caller has
    to interpret or, worse, a list rebuilt from the config naming steps nobody ran.
    """
    root = worktree_root(start)
    rec_path = os.path.join(scratch_dir(root), RECORD_NAME)
    sys.stdout.write(render_record(load_record(rec_path), git_sha("HEAD", root)))
    return EXIT_OK


def gate(start: str, after: str | None, list_only: bool,
         result: str | None = None, summary: str | None = None,
         fix_attempt: bool = False, fell_back: bool = False) -> int:
    # Discovery is seeded from the tree, not the caller's cwd, so the config that
    # supplies the steps and the directory they run in cannot come apart.
    root = worktree_root(start)
    config_path = find_config(root)
    cfg = load_mapping(config_path)
    gate_cfg = gate_map(cfg, config_path)
    steps = resolve_steps(gate_cfg, config_path)
    # Validated here rather than where the bound is applied, so `--list` (the
    # skill's first call) reports a bad budget before any step runs.
    budget = fix_budget(gate_cfg, config_path)

    if list_only:
        for step in steps:
            emit("list", step["name"], step["kind"], step["target"],
                 step["model"] or "-", step["when"])
        return EXIT_OK

    base = str(cfg.get("base") or "main")
    remote = str(cfg.get("remote") or "origin")
    scratch = scratch_dir(root)
    rec_path = os.path.join(scratch, RECORD_NAME)

    # Usage errors first, before anything looks at the record: whether these flags
    # make sense is a question about the arguments and the config, and answering it
    # after a restart has already dropped `--after` would swallow the mistake.
    if (result or summary or fell_back) and not after:
        raise ConfigError("--result, --summary and --fell-back describe the step named "
                          "by --after, which was not given")
    if (result or summary or fell_back) and next(
            (s["kind"] for s in steps if s["name"] == after), "skill") != "skill":
        raise ConfigError(f"--result, --summary and --fell-back report a `skill:` step's "
                          f"outcome, but {after!r} is a `run:` step the gate ran itself")
    if fell_back:
        # Falling back means an unreachable *pinned* model, so it is meaningless on an
        # unpinned step, and the report only renders the fallback where there is a pin
        # to name. Refuse it rather than record a flag the report would silently drop.
        pin = next((s["model"] for s in steps if s["name"] == after), None)
        # `pin == ""` is a named step with no pin; `None` is an unknown name that
        # `remaining_steps` reports below, so leave that to it.
        if pin == "":
            raise ConfigError(f"--fell-back reports the pinned model for {after!r} was "
                              f"unreachable, but it has no `model:` pin to fall back from")
    if fix_attempt and after:
        raise ConfigError("--fix-attempt marks a re-run of the whole gate after a fix, "
                          "but --after resumes the walk already in progress, which is "
                          "not a new attempt")

    todo = remaining_steps(steps, after)  # raises on an `--after` naming no step
    record = load_record(rec_path)

    spent = attempts_spent(record)
    if fix_attempt:
        if record is None:
            # Scratch was cleared, or this is a re-run of a gate whose record never
            # got written. Counting from zero over-credits by whatever was already
            # spent, but refusing to run would cost the gate itself, and losing
            # scratch state should cost accuracy rather than the ability to check.
            sys.stderr.write("sapa gate: no gate record to count fix attempts against; "
                             "treating this as the first fix of a new attempt\n")
        if spent >= budget:
            # The refusal, not just a warning: this is the case the budget exists for,
            # where the skill has read the attempts line and kept going anyway. Nothing
            # runs and no run is appended, so the account stays what actually happened.
            sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: {spent} fix attempts already spent on this "
                             f"gate and `max_fix_attempts` is {budget}; repeated fixes "
                             f"are not converging, so the failure is likely deeper than "
                             f"the patch. Hand it back to the developer\n")
            emit("attempts", spent, budget)
            return EXIT_ATTEMPTS_SPENT

    if after and not resumable(record, steps, after, git_sha("HEAD", root)):
        sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: no gate record covering the steps before {after!r}; "
                         f"walking from the first step so the record can't understate "
                         f"what ran\n")
        if result or summary or fell_back:
            # Said out loud because the alternative is a reported outcome vanishing:
            # the restart has no run to attach it to, and the walk that follows will
            # reach the step again and ask for the verdict a second time. `--fell-back`
            # is part of that outcome, so it goes with the restart too.
            sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: the reported outcome for {after!r} goes with "
                             f"it — report it again when the walk reaches that step\n")
        after, todo, result, summary, fell_back = None, steps, None, None, False

    if after is None:
        # A bare invocation is a new run, appended rather than replacing: #99 gates the
        # `run:` steps alone before pushing a CI fix, and it needs the run that had
        # the whole gate to still be here afterward.
        record = record or {"runs": []}
        record["runs"].append(new_run(root, base, remote, spent + 1 if fix_attempt else 0))
        save_record(rec_path, record)
    else:
        # Record the skill step the agent just handled, before walking on past it.
        handled = next(s for s in steps if s["name"] == after)
        if handled["kind"] == "skill":
            entry = skill_entry(handled, result, summary, fell_back)
            record["runs"][-1]["steps"].append(entry)
            save_record(rec_path, record)
            emit("step", handled["name"], "skill", entry["result"], "-")
            if entry["result"] == "fail":
                return fail_run(record, rec_path, root, budget)

    if any(s["kind"] == "skill" for s in todo):
        path, state = materialize_plan(root, scratch)
        record["runs"][-1]["spec_source"] = state
        save_record(rec_path, record)
        emit("plan", path, "present" if state == "present" else "absent")

    changed = None  # computed on the first `run:` step, so a skill-only walk skips git
    for step in todo:
        if step["kind"] == "skill":
            emit("needs-skill", step["name"], step["target"], step["model"] or "-")
            return EXIT_NEEDS_SKILL
        if changed is None:
            changed = changed_files(remote, base, root)
        entry = run_step(step, root, base, changed)
        record["runs"][-1]["steps"].append(entry)
        save_record(rec_path, record)
        if entry["exit"] != 0:
            return fail_run(record, rec_path, root, budget)

    finish_run(record, rec_path, root, "green")
    emit("done", "green")
    return EXIT_OK


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="sapa gate",
        add_help=True,
        description=__doc__,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
    )
    parser.add_argument("--after", metavar="NAME",
                        help="resume with the step after NAME (the skill step just handled)")
    parser.add_argument("--result", choices=("pass", "fail"),
                        help="with --after: the outcome the skill step reported "
                             "(default pass, since resuming means it passed)")
    parser.add_argument("--summary", metavar="TEXT",
                        help="with --after: one line on what the skill step found")
    parser.add_argument("--fell-back", action="store_true",
                        help="with --after: the step's pinned model was unreachable, "
                             "so it ran on the session model; the report says so "
                             "instead of asserting the pin ran")
    parser.add_argument("--fix-attempt", action="store_true",
                        help="this run re-checks a fix sapa applied itself; counts "
                             "against `gate.max_fix_attempts` and is refused once "
                             "the budget is spent")
    parser.add_argument("--list", action="store_true", dest="list_only",
                        help="print the resolved steps and exit, running nothing")
    parser.add_argument("--report", action="store_true",
                        help="print the `## Gates` section for the recorded run, "
                             "running nothing")
    parser.add_argument("--start", default=os.environ.get("PWD") or os.getcwd(),
                        help="resolve the worktree from this directory instead of $PWD")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    try:
        if args.report:
            return report(os.path.abspath(args.start))
        return gate(os.path.abspath(args.start), args.after, args.list_only,
                    args.result, args.summary, args.fix_attempt, args.fell_back)
    except (ConfigError, ProjectConfigError) as exc:
        sys.stderr.write(f"sapa gate: {exc}\n")
        return EXIT_CONFIG


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
